--- Rosetta Code: Break OO privacy
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Break_OO_privacy
--- Crow has no classes and no private members, so there is no privacy to break: a record's
--- fields are read and written wherever the record is, a leading underscore marks a field
--- private by convention only, and a module of the program's own is reached whole through
--- its prefix. (The one privacy Crow keeps, the standard modules' own helpers, has no escape
--- hatch: `math._one[1]` is refused with "math has no '_one'".) A debugger or a serializer
--- reads a record whole: `log` and `str` show every field, `type` names the record, and
--- `encode` writes it as JSON, whose keys a program walks by name.

struct Account [owner, _balance, _pin]

fn open[owner, deposit]
    return Account[owner, deposit, 1234]

--- what the account means to show of itself
fn statement[a]
    return '`a.owner` holds an account'

a = open['Ada', 250]
log[statement[a]]

--- the fields meant to be private, read from outside
log['balance `a._balance`, PIN `a._pin`']

--- and written: the record held by a is a fresh one with the field changed
a._balance = 1000000
log['balance now `a._balance`']

--- as a debugger shows it
log['`type[a]`: `str[a]`']

--- as a serializer sees it: every field, found by name
fields = json[encode[a]]
for name in keys[fields]
    log['  `name` = `fields[name]`']
